A||N||11||Monkeyman||How do you rebuild a motor????||||5
Z||000000||Monkeyman||11-28-1999||12:35 AM||||I'm gonna rebuild/upgrade my motor myself.  (2.8 into a 3.1 or 3.2)  I've never done anything like this before.  I assume I can get some kind of manual to explain the basic steps, but I have a couple of specific questions:  <BR>     Where can I go to find out what specific parts I need to do a 3.1 or 3.2?  Most of the mechanics won't even do the job themselves which leads me to believe that they don't have any idea what to change.<BR>     What things do I need to send out?  I know that some things have to be done to the heads and the block, but I'm not sure exactly what they are.<BR>     Is there a web site out there somewhere that would explain all of this to me?  I know I could find all the answers here on the forum, but since I need to be so specific, I don't want to take up anyones' time.  I don't want to ask any mechanics around here because: 1. I don't think they have any idea. and 2. I don't want to spend any more money than I have to.  I have an '86 2m4 I can drive while I'm working on the motor in my '85 GT so it doesn't matter how long it takes to get the job done.  HELP!!<BR>  ||||reg||5
Z||000001||fierospeeder||11-28-1999||01:15 AM|||| <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/001174.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/001174.html</A> <BR>heres a link to an old discussion we had about someone doing this to their fiero.  Im interested also.  I heard the v60 rebuild book has a chapter in it, the fiero store sells the book.||||reg||1
Z||000002||mwbackus||11-28-1999||10:29 AM||||Have rebuilt quite a few motors myself in the past, I dont think I would suggest it to one who has no prior experience doing it. Just the tools alone required to do it properly would justify having it done by a professional.  Of course, this is just my opinion, everyone has to start somewhere. :-)<P>Mike<BR>(Fiero info in my user profile)||||reg||1
Z||000003||batboy||11-28-1999||10:33 AM||||The standard 2.8/3.1 overbore size is 0.030" over, but you can go 0.040" over. If I were doing it, I'd go 0.030" for a margin of safety (assuming it needs punched out). Most good machine shops want you to bring in the new set of pitons too, so they can bore and hone the cylinders to perfectly match the set of pistons you'll be using. So, the first step is to locate and purchase the pistons. Remember, if you are stroking it, you must buy 3.1 oversized pistons to work with the 3.1 crank.<P>Oh yeah, when the block goes in to the shop, make sure the main caps are on it. Also, have the shop magnaflux (to check for cracks) and clean the block, then install new cam bearings at the same time as you get it bored out. They probably should check the deck for warpage too and you may need to mill the deck slightly so the heads gaskets will seal good.<P>Head need to go into the machine shop too. They need magnafluxed, cleaned, usually new valve guides, new springs if this is a high performance rebuild, and the valves and seats checked and reground. The mounting surface needs checked for straightness and maybe milled to remove any warpage.<P>To stroke the 2.8 into a 3.1 (3.2 if bored out) you need to buy a 3.1 crank, 3.1 pistons to match the bore, and a neutrally balanced flywheel. You mentioned in another thread that you got a $1500 quote to rebuild the engine from a mechanic. Even doing all the labor yourself, if you buy top name parts and do everything right, plus add in the machine shop charges, a 3.1 stroker high performance rebuild will probably run about that much. Most auto machine shops will reassemble the short block for a reasonable fee, in most cases, I'd trust them with my engine before I would a regular mechanic.<P>Rebuilding an engine is almost an art form. It is a time consuming labor of love. Keep everything clean, always use a torque wrench, the heads bolt on using a special torquing pattern, don't force anything, use a ring compressor when putting the pistons in, lubricate all moving parts when reassembling, and always check all your clearances when you're putting it back together. It's a lot of work, good luck.||||reg||1
Z||000004||RFMike||11-28-1999||06:32 PM||||I would suggest you follow the path I am going to take, which is purchasing a long block and installing it.  I am working on pricing right now.  I have a friend that works at Big Wheel.  This retailer also sells remanufactured engines.  A long block bored to .040 with a performance cam is $1,155, which includes shipping.  He is working on the price with a 3.1 crank, with the correct pistons, etc.; I'll know by Tuesday or Wednesday.  The remanufacturer is Recon, located in Philadelphia.  The Warranty is through Big Wheel, and it is 36 months / 36,00 miles.  <BR>Anybody know anything about them? <A HREF="http://www.reconengines.com/ae.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.reconengines.com/ae.html</A> <BR>Big Wheel also sells Global Engines, and a host of others.  Anybody have any remanufacturer recommendations?<BR>This option will give you less control over what parts go into the rebuild, but if you have never done anything like rebuilding an engine, this is a more realistic goal, unless you have access to a lot of help.||||reg||1
Z||000005||batboy||11-28-1999||08:41 PM||||That's a great idea, Mike, especially for the novice. A major rebuild it not an easy task. I'd rather go with a reman short block though, especially if they will give you the choice of performance cams. I'd rather use and have the machine shop rebuild the old Fiero HO heads.<P>I wonder how careful those reman shops are about putting the correct cast iron HO heads on a Fiero 2.8/3.1 engine. Once it's assembled, you can't measure the valve size. Also, I like to have my machine shop do some pocket porting during the head rebuild. This is always good for some extra flow and a few more ponies.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Monkeyman||11-28-1999||10:12 PM||||Batboy--what's "pocket porting"?  If I got a short block (maybe remaned), could I still go with a 3.2?  How much more would a 3.2 cost over a 3.1?  What would I gain and does anybody think it would be worth it.  Also, I know that the 3.1 was a stock motor in other cars so I'm sure that replacement parts would be easy to come by.  What about parts for a 3.2?||||reg||5
Z||000007||RFMike||11-28-1999||11:34 PM||||Batboy,<BR>I had the same thought about the heads, and told my friend to ask specifically about the valve size.  They definitely are Fiero heads.  As for porting, yea, I know.  There are a lot of things I could do, but in the big picture, this should be a big jump in performance.  I just don't have the time to take it all on myself.  I will be installing a set of headers (maybe the stainless steel ones from Fieroshop, headers only), hopefully I get back any mileage I lose from the swap.<P>M-Man, most of the remanufacturers I have looked at do a .040 overbore, as part of the price.  That is where the extra displacement comes from.  Parts (outside of the combustion chamber) are the same as a 3.1.||||reg||1
Z||000008||batboy||11-29-1999||10:07 AM||||Mike, if those are indeed Fiero heads, then it sounds like a pretty good deal with minimal fuss. Can you keep your old HO heads?<P>Monkey, pocket porting the heads is a hotrodder's method of grinding and smoothing the intake and exhaust runners down in the bowl area (just below the valve seat), it helps head flow a little, especially at high RPM. With the V6 heads, leave that little "vane" alone, that helps straighten out the flow. If you or your machinist don't know how to do this procedure, you are better not attempting it, you don't want to hit the water jacket and ruin a good set of heads.<P>In about 1990 or so, GM added a crank with a longer stroke and pistons to match, into the 2.8 V6 engine to make it a 3.1 size. This engine bored out to either 0.030" or 0.040" increases the displacement approximately to 3.2 liters. I think all reman engines are bored out to the next standard oversize.<P>A 3.1 engine with cast iron head from a early 1990's minivan is a near perfect bolt in swap (if you use the Fiero 2.8 intake, pulleys, belts, water pump, and other externals), except it has a low performance cam and econo heads with small valves. One of these 3.1 short blocks with the Fiero HO heads and a better cam would be a fairly cheap route to go too.||||reg||1
Z||000009||RFMike||11-29-1999||07:48 PM||||No, they all want the heads back.  In fact, if you don't return the heads, they nick you for 50% of the core refund.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Carrolles||11-30-1999||07:46 AM||||FOCOA ran a four part article on stroking the 2.8L to 3.1L that is excellent.  It tells what parts to get, what machine work to have done, and the order in  which to do it.  It has plenty of pictures also.  I have the 60 degree V-6 engine rebuild book but found the FOCOA article more help.  <P>I believe you can order back issues.||||reg||1
